{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>The young men of the children of Israel</b> — these are the firstborn, according to the words of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 24:5), for they are the ones who offer the burnt offerings and the peace offerings. And I do not know why he would designate the firstborn with the term נַעֲרֵי (young men); perhaps because he mentioned the elders, who are the nobles of the children of Israel, he called the firstborn נְעָרִים, for they are young men relative to them. This hints that it was not on account of their excellence in wisdom that he sent them, for they were not elders, but rather on account of the birthright, for they are those consecrated for the sacrifices. And according to the plain sense, נַעֲרֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל (the young men of the children of Israel) are the choice young men of Israel who have not tasted the taste of sin, who have never approached a woman, for they are the select of the people and the most holy among them, as in that which they said (Berakhot 43b): \"The choice young men of Israel who have not tasted the taste of sin are destined to give forth a fragrance like Lebanon,\" etc." "And the sense of <b>unto YHWH, oxen</b> is that Israel, all the days they were in the wilderness, were in fear of the attribute of justice, and this was their error in the matter of the calf. Now, they offered the burnt offerings and slaughtered the peace offerings — all of them oxen — in the manner of the bull of the anointed priest (Leviticus 4:3) and the bull for a matter hidden from sight (ibid. v. 14) and the bull for idolatry (Numbers 15:24); likewise the red heifer (ibid. 19:2) is so too. I will elaborate on this matter in connection with the calf (Ramban on Exodus 32:1)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:15:36.464438161Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}